{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 35, :comments ["<b>And all his daughters.</b> Rabbi Yehudah says: twins were born with the tribes, and they married them. Rabbi Nehemiah says: they were Canaanitesses. Rather, what is 'and all his daughters'? His daughters-in-law, for it is a person's way to call his daughter-in-law his daughter. And there is a difficulty, for 'בת איש כנעני' is translated 'בת גבר תגרא' — and how is it possible? Abraham commanded Eliezer: 'לא תקח אשה לבני מבנות הכנעני,' and Isaac commanded Jacob: 'לא תקח אשה לבני מבנות הכנעני,' and yet Judah went and married a Canaanitess? Rather, what is 'בת איש כנעני'? A merchant's daughter. And one may answer that that translation is according to Rabbi Yehudah, who says twins were born with the tribes, etc. And so it appears from what is written: 'ושאול בן הכנענית' — implying that the others were not Canaanitesses. But according to Rabbi Nehemiah, there was no need to translate 'בת איש כנעני' as 'גבר תגרא' — rather, a Canaanite literally. However, there is a difficulty according to Rabbi Yehudah, for we say: Abraham our father fulfilled even the command of eruv tavshilin, and also the mitzvah of yibum they would fulfill, as it is written: 'בא אל אשת אחיך ויבם אותה' — and if so, how could the tribes have married their sisters? And also Jacob, how could he have married two sisters during their lifetimes? And one may answer that since they were not commanded concerning the Torah, even though it was known through ruach ha-kodesh, whatever they wished to fulfill they fulfilled, and whatever they wished to leave they left. And that which we say in [the chapter] ערבי פסחים — that at the feast of the righteous in the time to come, the cup of blessing is given to bless Jacob, and he says, 'I will not bless,' because he married two sisters during their lifetimes — this implies that he did improperly. One may answer that nevertheless, since the Torah was destined to be given, there is some punishment in the matter. And even so, he married them despite there being some punishment, because he wanted only righteous women, and he did not find in that generation righteous women like them, and also one of them alone could not have borne all twelve tribes." "<b>And his father wept for him.</b> Rashi explains: Isaac was weeping over Jacob's trouble, but he was not mourning, for he knew that he was alive. And he did not wish to reveal it to Jacob, for the honor of the Shechinah, as we explained above regarding one who has taken a vow of herem and has associated the Holy One, blessed be He, with them — not to reveal the matter. And even though it is written: 'מגיד דבריו ליעקב,' he did not reveal it because of the herem. And when they went down to Egypt and found him alive, then they permitted the herem, and the Shechinah rested upon him, as it is written: 'ותחי רוח יעקב אביהם' — implying that until now the Shechinah had not rested upon him."], :book :genesis, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 37, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:06:44.832310156Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}